+From: milkman-wang <109866905+milkman-wang@users.noreply.github.com>
+Subject: [PATCH] nft_fullcone: honour the port range and the flags
+
+nft_fullcone_init() never read NFTA_FULLCONE_FLAGS or the proto range
+registers, so rules such as "fullcone to :1024-2047" or "fullcone random"
+worked like a plain "fullcone": the external port was chosen from
+1024-65535 whatever the range said. The attribute numbers also differ
+from the ones the libnftnl and nftables fullcone patches send
+(FLAGS = 1, REG_PROTO_MIN = 2, REG_PROTO_MAX = 3), so reading them as
+numbered here would mix them up.
+
+Number the attributes like userspace does, and parse them in
+nft_fullcone_init() the way nft_masq does.
+
+The range from the rule then reaches code that so far only saw
+1024-65535:
+
+- find_appropriate_port{,6}() kept the range size and the loop counter
+  in 16 bits, so ":0-65535" gave a size of 0: a division by zero with
+  random or fully-random, port 0 without. Use 32 bits, and swap a
+  reversed range like nf_nat_l4proto_unique_tuple() does.
+
+- nf_nat_handle_postrouting() reused an active UDP mapping whatever its
+  external port, so after a range change, or with different ranges for
+  different destinations, a new flow could get a port outside the
+  rule's range. The lookups by internal source now take the port range.
+  One socket can then have a mapping per range on the same external
+  address, so nf_nat_fullcone_handle_dying_tuples() also matches the
+  external port of a dying conntrack, to release the mapping it
+  belongs to.
+
+Signed-off-by: milkman-wang <109866905+milkman-wang@users.noreply.github.com>
